# ClarVar: Open-Source Variant Annotation & Prioritisation Pipeline

<div align="center">

[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Python 3.10+](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.10+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org)
[![Tests](https://img.shields.io/badge/tests-62%20passing-brightgreen.svg)](#running-tests)
[![Status: Alpha](https://img.shields.io/badge/Status-Alpha-orange.svg)](https://github.com/NosakhareOsaro/ClarVar)

**A lightweight, reproducible pipeline for annotating and prioritising genetic variants from clinical sequencing data.**

[Features](#features) · [Quick Start](#quick-start) · [How It Works](#how-it-works) · [Data Sources](#data-sources) · [Contributing](#contributing)

</div>

---

## Overview

Clinical sequencing can produce **thousands of variants per sample**, but identifying the few that matter remains a major challenge. ClarVar addresses this by providing a lightweight, open-source pipeline that integrates:

- **Consequence prediction** via [Ensembl VEP](https://www.ensembl.org/vep) REST API (frameshift, missense, splice sites, and more)
- **Population allele frequencies** from [gnomAD v4.1](https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org/) (730,947 exomes + 76,215 genomes on GRCh38)
- **Clinical evidence** from [ClinVar](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/clinvar/) (pathogenicity classifications and disease associations)
- **In silico pathogenicity scores** — CADD PHRED, SIFT, and PolyPhen-2
- **Evidence-based priority scoring** that ranks variants by composite clinical importance

ClarVar requires only Python and an internet connection. No local VEP installation, no database downloads, no HPC cluster needed to get started.

---

## Features

- Parses `.vcf` and `.vcf.gz` files; handles multi-allelic sites and skips structural variants
- Batched VEP REST API calls (200 variants/request) with automatic rate-limit handling
- Configurable filters: gnomAD AF ceiling, CADD floor, consequence whitelist
- Three built-in ranking strategies (default, rarity-first, clinical-evidence-first)
- TSV output + self-contained HTML report with ClinVar badges and OMIM links
- JSON summary report with consequence and ClinVar distributions
- Clean Python API for programmatic access and custom workflows
- 62 unit tests; live API integration test suite (opt-in)

---

## Quick Start

### Installation

```bash
git clone https://github.com/NosakhareOsaro/ClarVar.git
cd ClarVar
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Verify the install:

```bash
clarvar --version
pytest tests/ -v        # 62 tests should pass
```

### Run the example

```bash
clarvar pipeline -i data/sample/sample_variants.vcf -o results/ --verbose
```

This produces four files in `results/`:

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `prioritised.vcf` | Annotated VCF with CADD, gnomAD AF, ClinVar in INFO fields |
| `prioritised_variants.tsv` | Ranked tab-separated table, one variant per row |
| `report.json` | JSON summary with top variants and distributions |
| `report.html` | Self-contained HTML report — open in any browser |

### CLI reference

All commands write outputs into the directory given by `-o` (created automatically).

```bash
# Full pipeline — annotation + prioritisation + all four output files
clarvar pipeline -i input.vcf -o results/

# Annotate only (writes annotated.vcf into the output directory)
clarvar annotate -i raw.vcf -o results/

# Prioritise a pre-annotated VCF
clarvar prioritize -i annotated.vcf -o results/
```

**Available flags:**

| Flag | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `-i / --input` | required | Input VCF file (`.vcf` or `.vcf.gz`) |
| `-o / --output` | required | Output directory (created if it does not exist) |
| `-a / --assembly` | `GRCh38` | Genome assembly — `GRCh38` or `GRCh37` |
| `--no-html` | off | Skip HTML report (useful in automated/HPC pipelines) |
| `--top-n N` | `50` | Number of variants shown in the HTML report |
| `-v / --verbose` | off | Print progress and debug logging |

```bash
# GRCh37 assembly
clarvar pipeline -i patient.vcf -o results/ -a GRCh37

# Skip HTML generation
clarvar pipeline -i patient.vcf -o results/ --no-html

# Show top 100 variants in the HTML report
clarvar pipeline -i patient.vcf -o results/ --top-n 100
```

---

## Python API

```python
from clarvar.vcf_parser import VCFParser
from clarvar.annotator import VariantAnnotator
from clarvar.prioritizer import VariantPrioritizer
from clarvar.reporter import write_tsv, write_html_report
from pathlib import Path

# 1. Parse
parser = VCFParser()
variants = parser.parse_file("patient.vcf")
print(f"Parsed {len(variants)} variants")

# 2. Annotate (calls Ensembl VEP REST API)
annotator = VariantAnnotator(assembly="GRCh38")
annotated = annotator.annotate_collection(variants, verbose=True)

# 3. Prioritise
prioritizer = VariantPrioritizer()           # balanced default weights
prioritized = prioritizer.prioritize_collection(annotated)

# 4. Output
write_tsv(prioritized, Path("results/variants.tsv"))
write_html_report(prioritized.top_n(50), Path("results/report.html"))

report = prioritizer.generate_report(prioritized)
print(f"Top variant: {report['top_variants'][0]}")
```

### Alternative ranking strategies

```python
from clarvar.prioritizer import RarityFirstStrategy, ClinicalEvidenceFirstStrategy

# Emphasise rare variants (high frequency weight)
ranked = RarityFirstStrategy().rank(list(annotated))

# Emphasise ClinVar-classified variants
ranked = ClinicalEvidenceFirstStrategy().rank(list(annotated))
```

### GRCh37 support

```python
annotator = VariantAnnotator(assembly="GRCh37")
```

### Offline / HPC annotation

```python
from clarvar.annotator import LocalAnnotator

la = LocalAnnotator(db_path="/data/annotation_db")
la.load_databases()   # implement loading from local VCF/TSV databases
annotated = la.annotate_collection(variants)
```

---

## How It Works

### Pipeline

```
Input VCF (.vcf / .vcf.gz)
        │
        ▼
[VCFParser]  ── parse variants, normalise chromosomes, skip SVs
        │
        ▼
[VariantAnnotator]  ── batch POST to Ensembl VEP REST API
        │                 ├── consequence + HGVS notation
        │                 ├── CADD PHRED / SIFT / PolyPhen-2
        │                 ├── gnomAD v4.1 allele frequency
        │                 └── ClinVar clinical significance
        ▼
[VariantPrioritizer]  ── filter + score + sort
        │
        ▼
[Reporter]  ── TSV table  +  HTML report  +  JSON summary
```

### Priority score (0–100)

| Component | Weight | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Consequence severity | 40% | Frameshift/stop = 100, missense = 40, synonymous = 10 |
| ClinVar significance | 40% | Pathogenic = 100, VUS = 30, benign = 5 |
| Population frequency | 20% | AF < 0.01% → 100, AF > 10% → 20 |

**Final score** = 0.4 × consequence + 0.4 × ClinVar + 0.2 × frequency

ClinVar-pathogenic variants are never filtered out by consequence filters, regardless of their VEP consequence term.

---

## Data Sources

| Source | What it provides | Version / Release |
|---|---|---|
| [Ensembl VEP REST](https://rest.ensembl.org) | Consequence, CADD, SIFT, PolyPhen | v115 (GRCh38), v111 (GRCh37) |
| [gnomAD](https://gnomad.broadinstitute.org) | Population allele frequencies | v4.1 (730,947 exomes, 76,215 genomes) |
| [ClinVar](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/clinvar/) | Clinical significance | Weekly release |

All sources are publicly available and require no data access agreement.

**References:**
1. McLaren W et al. (2016) The Ensembl Variant Effect Predictor. *Genome Biology*, 17:122.
2. Karczewski KJ et al. (2020) The mutational constraint spectrum from variation in 141,456 humans. *Nature*, 581:434–443.
3. Landrum MJ et al. (2018) ClinVar: improving access to variant interpretations. *Nucleic Acids Research*, 46:D1062–D1067.
4. Rentzsch P et al. (2019) CADD: predicting the deleteriousness of variants throughout the human genome. *Nucleic Acids Research*, 47:D886–D894.
5. Richards S et al. (2015) Standards and guidelines for the interpretation of sequence variants. *Genetics in Medicine*, 17:405–423.

